							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=117.msg1702#msg1702">Quote from: my name is eric. on October 18, 2006, 07:09:09 pm</a></div><div class="quote">i thought the show sucked. ice t is embarrassing for hip hop... AND i thought the way the whole fucking show glorified being a gangster was dumb too. boo!<br /></div><br />Eric - lol - your WAY too young to know that thats what the 80s and 90s in Hiphop was ABOUT.&nbsp; Its not gangster rap like it is nowadays - back in the day it was DIFFERENT.&nbsp; You need to buy a NWA album and listen to it well.&nbsp; It was part of history and I loved that group....<br /><br />It wasnt always conscious but it was always TRUTH - Hiphop evolves like everything else.&nbsp; And you need to do your history to be able to appreciate shows like this.<br /><br />SAM - oh don&#039;t get me wrong.&nbsp; I TOTALLy agree with you as far as the back asswards order they are honored artists in.&nbsp; The fact that Rakim or Bam werent in the first 2 shows, was wack.&nbsp; I also agree with the whole graf element being barely featured.&nbsp; I wonder how much of the odd placement and order of all of the elements is a factor of availability and people actually wanting to be part of it.&nbsp; I guess we will never know.<br /><br />And you are NOT nerding up the post!&nbsp; I think your intelligence on this helps these young kids really GET what these honors are about - and plants these older names into their heads, to hopefully do some research.<br /><br />Donuts and Eric - while I understand your sentiment toward the show - the fact that you didn&#039;t enjoy it because it was too &quot;gangsta&quot; is just silly if you knew those artists history and contribution to Hiphop history on the whole.&nbsp; Their music may not be whats popular NOW - but its classic and was meaningful to a generation of people, just like yourselves at one point.<br /><br />Before you